---
import PathwayLandingLayout from '../../layouts/PathwayLandingLayout.astro';
import { getPathwayCategoryHero } from '@/lib/pathwayHeroes';
import { SITE_CONFIG } from '@/lib/seo/config';

const title = 'EB-5 vs. E-2, L-1, O-1, and Global Residency Options | StudentEB5';
const metaDescription =
  'EB-5 compared with E-2, L-1, and O-1 U.S. visa categories, plus a high-level look at how global residency programs differ in goals from a U.S. EB-5 green card path.';
const canonicalUrl = `${SITE_CONFIG.url}/pathways/eb5-vs-other-visa-options`;
const h1 = 'EB-5 vs. E-2, L-1, O-1, and Global Residency Options';
const subhead =
  'How EB-5’s investment-based, employer-independent green card path differs from common U.S. nonimmigrant categories, and from other countries’ residency programs.';

const relatedResearch = [
  {
    title: 'Golden Visas vs EB-5 for Elite Students',
    url: '/research/golden-visas-vs-eb5-elite-students',
    description: 'Deeper golden-visa vs EB-5 framing for student and family planning.',
  },
  {
    title: 'EB-5 for L-1 Visa Holders',
    url: '/pathways/eb5-for-l1',
    description: 'L-1-specific framing when evaluating EB-5 as a permanent path.',
  },
];
const hero = getPathwayCategoryHero('visa-comparison');

---

<PathwayLandingLayout
  title={title}
  metaDescription={metaDescription}
  canonicalUrl={canonicalUrl}
  h1={h1}
  subhead={subhead}
  heroImage={hero.image}
  heroImageWebp={hero.webp}
  heroImageAlt="EB-5 pathway comparing EB-5 and other visa options"
  relatedResearch={relatedResearch}
  secondaryCtaText="Talk to Someone About Comparing Visa and Residency Paths"
  breadcrumbTitle="EB-5 vs. Other Visa Options"
  keywords={[
    'EB-5 vs E-2',
    'EB-5 vs L-1',
    'EB-5 vs O-1',
    'EB-5 vs golden visa',
  ]}
>
  <h2>EB-5 vs. other U.S. visa categories</h2>
  <div class="not-prose grid md:grid-cols-1 gap-6 mb-6">
    <div class="rounded-lg border border-border bg-card p-6">
      <h3 class="font-serif text-xl font-semibold text-foreground mb-3">E-2 (treaty investor)</h3>
      <p class="text-muted-foreground leading-relaxed m-0" style="line-height: 1.75;">
        Nonimmigrant treaty-investor status. Generally requires nationality of a treaty country, an investment in a U.S.
        business, and ongoing E-2 compliance. It is not itself a direct green card path. EB-5, by contrast, is an
        immigrant category aimed at permanent residence through a qualifying EB-5 investment structure. Different legal
        framework, different petition forms, and different outcome (immigrant vs. temporary treaty status).
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="rounded-lg border border-border bg-card p-6">
      <h3 class="font-serif text-xl font-semibold text-foreground mb-3">L-1 (intracompany transfer)</h3>
      <p class="text-muted-foreground leading-relaxed m-0" style="line-height: 1.75;">
        Employer-dependent nonimmigrant status for qualifying intracompany transferees. Time-limited and tied to the
        petitioning employer relationship. EB-5 does not require that same continuing employer transfer eligibility; it
        is an investment-based immigrant petition. L-1 holders sometimes evaluate EB-5 as a permanent alternative.
        See <a href="/pathways/eb5-for-l1">EB-5 for L-1 visa holders</a>.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="rounded-lg border border-border bg-card p-6">
      <h3 class="font-serif text-xl font-semibold text-foreground mb-3">O-1 (extraordinary ability)</h3>
      <p class="text-muted-foreground leading-relaxed m-0" style="line-height: 1.75;">
        Nonimmigrant category for individuals who meet a high, specific evidentiary bar for extraordinary ability in
        defined fields. No EB-5-style capital investment is required, but the proof standard is demanding and distinct.
        EB-5 turns on investment, source of funds, and job creation rather than an O-1-style ability showing. Different
        eligibility theory entirely.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div
    class="not-prose rounded-lg border-2 border-foreground/20 bg-secondary/50 p-5 mb-8"
    role="note"
  >
    <p class="text-sm text-muted-foreground leading-relaxed m-0" style="line-height: 1.7;">
      These summaries are educational. Category rules, treaty lists, and evidentiary standards change. None of these
      paths is ranked here as generally preferable. Fit depends on nationality, career evidence, capital, and goals.
      Confirm options with an immigration attorney.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2>EB-5 vs. global residency options</h2>
  <p>
    Some investors also look at programs outside the United States (for example, Canada permanent residence pathways or
    Portugal’s Golden Visa) while considering EB-5. Those programs and U.S. EB-5 serve different goals: residency and
    physical-presence rules differ, paths to citizenship differ, and investment or selection structures differ. This page
    does not rank them or restate current program thresholds that go stale. For deeper golden-visa vs EB-5 framing aimed
    at student and family planning, see
    <a href="/research/golden-visas-vs-eb5-elite-students">golden visas vs EB-5 for elite students</a>.
  </p>
</PathwayLandingLayout>
